    "lawCode": "Amd §613, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S4645\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the public health law, in relation\nto requiring parents of children in certain child care settings\nreceive educational materials on influenza disease and influenza\nimmunizations\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL : To ensure that parents of children\nin certain child care settings receive information outlining the\nbenefits of influenza immunizations\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS : Section 1 of the bill provides that\nthe commissioner shall administer a program of influenza education and\noutreach to parents and guardians of children ages six months to fifty\nnine months who attend certain childcare programs. The commissioner,\nin collaboration with the office of children and family services\n(OCFS) and the department of education shall consult with child care\nproviders to distribute educational materials annually.\n\nSection 2 of the bill provides the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :  In 2007, the Advisory Committee on Immunization\nPractices (ACIP) made changes to the childhood immunization schedule\nthat included recommendations that all children aged six months to\nfifty-nine months receive a yearly influenza vaccine. Influenza is a\nhighly contagious respiratory illness. Each year in the United States,\nbetween five and twenty percent of the population is infected with\ninfluenza and approximately 36,000 people die and more than 200,000\npeople are hospitalized due to complications from influenza. Certain\npopulations, such as young children, are most vulnerable. Given the\nlarge numbers of children in child care, it is important to prevent\nthe spread of this illness with yearly vaccinations. The best time to\nreceive the vaccine is in October or November. However, getting\nvaccinated in December or later can still be beneficial because most\ninfluenza outbreaks occur in January or later most years. This bill\nwould ensure that parents of children aged six months to fifty-nine\nmonths in certain child care settings receive valuable information on\ninfluenza disease and the benefits of annual influenza immunizations.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY : A.8910A, 2007 and 2008 passed Assembly.\nSame as S.5118B, 2007 and 2008 referred to rules.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS : Minimal.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE : April 20, 2010.\n",
