    "lawCode": "Amd §613, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S4645A\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the public health law, in relation to providing\nparents of children ages six months to eighteen years of age who\nattend licensed and registered day care programs, nursery schools,\npre-kindergarten, kindergarten, school age child care programs, public\nschools or non-public schools with educational materials on influenza\ndisease and influenza immunizations\n\n\n GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\nTo ensure that parents of children in certain child care settings,\npublic schools or non-public schools receive information outlining the\nbenefits of influenza immunizations\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\nSection 1 of the bill provides that the commissioner shall administer\na program of influenza education and outreach to parents and guardians\nof children ages six months to eighteen years of age who attend\ncertain childcare programs, public schools or non-public schools. The\ndepartment of health (DOH) shall make educational materials regarding\ninfluenza and the benefits of influenza immunizations available on the\ndepartment's website. The office of children and family services, the\ndepartment of education, and the New York City department of Health\nand mental hygiene shall notify such childcare programs, public\nschools or nonpublic schools that information is available on DOH's\nwebsite and that the information should be posted in plain view in\nanticipation of times of highest risk for contraction of influenza as\ndetermined by the commissioner of health. \"Organizations of child care\nresource and referral agencies\" is added to the list of groups from\nwhich the commissioner shall seek assistance and cooperation in such\neducation and outreach program.\n\nSection 2 of the bill provides the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nIn 2007, the Advisory Committee on Immunization Practices (ACIP) made\nchanges to the childhood immunization schedule that included\nrecommendations that all children aged six months to fifty-nine months\nreceive a yearly influenza vaccine. Influenza is a highly contagious\nrespiratory illness. That information was again updated to include\nchildren up to age eighteen. Each year in the United States, between\nfive and twenty percent of the population is infected with influenza\nand approximately 36,000 people die and more than 200,000 people are\nhospitalized due to complications from influenza. Certain populations,\nsuch as young children, are most vulnerable. Given the large numbers\nof children who are in close proximity to one another in child care\ncenters and schools, it is important to prevent the spread of\ninfluenza with yearly vaccinations. The best time to receive the\nvaccine is in October or November. However, getting vaccinated in\nDecember or later can still be beneficial because most influenza\noutbreaks often occur in January or later. This bill would ensure that\nparents of children aged six months to eighteen years of age receive\nvaluable information on influenza disease and the benefits of annual\ninfluenza immunizations.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\nA.8910A, 2007 and 2008 passed Assembly.  Same as S.5118B, 2007 and\n2008 referred to rules.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS :\nVirtually none; the cost of providing information on an already\nexisting public website and disseminating it through already existing\nchannels.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect April 20, 2010. Effective immediately, the\naddition, amendment and/or repeal of any rule or regulation necessary\nfor the implementation of this act on its effective date are\nauthorized and directed to be made and completed on or before such\neffective date.\n",
